#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065326} :activity/id #uuid "35485642-6f34-523f-ba9d-b8883802af55" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049168 :topic/id #uuid "e02cd9a7-60ac-54e8-865a-ffe6fe24b944" :topic/math-academy-id 369} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065177 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064288 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064128 :question/id #uuid "ceddd310-5fe4-5b55-8210-991ff22c66b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7349"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "253c949d-e223-5124-ae37-c268d2ad9a2b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554585}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of an Angle as a Leg Varies"} :step/id #uuid "9ce63c6b-93ff-55b3-823f-2b9e85238652" :step/math-academy-id 27273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065387 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a jet takes off and climbs with an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians to the horizontal. The speed of the jet is $462\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}$ and the angle of elevation does not change. How fast is the altitude of the jet increasing?\n\nLet's begin by drawing a diagram depicting the given information.\n\n![](images/70/708ba0a80dd7c8e3e58e1456ad643338a8e630ec45aedb8955b042ddac92c329.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the jet's height from the ground (i.e., its altitude), $z$ is the jet's distance from its take-off point. The quantity that we're required to calculate is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 462\\,\\textrm{km/h},$ so plugging this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 462 = 231.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the altitude is increasing at a rate of $231\\,\\textrm{km/h}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "da3b2021-8bcc-506c-8874-9ce60aca7594" :tutorial/math-academy-id 501 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d3981e94-4f89-5b37-84f2-1d3e0cbb41c1" :step/math-academy-id 934 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065327}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064385 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064029 :question/id #uuid "a669adb9-c12e-520d-994b-cddf4f9fedb6" :question/math-academy-id "e-233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "275009c1-6d24-5677-85a3-ec734133ac52" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049658}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554593}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg or the Hypotenuse at a Fixed Angle"} :step/id #uuid "72b9ba59-522f-50d2-bed7-143439adb60f" :step/math-academy-id 935 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065328}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065388 :tutorial/content "Consider a $2\\,\\text{m}$ ladder that leans against a wall, forming an angle $\\theta$ with the ground. The bottom of the ladder is pushed toward the wall at a rate of $\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ as shown below. How fast is the angle $\\theta$ increasing?\n\nLet's begin by drawing a figure according to the given information.\n\n![](images/16/16996176a8557dc71fe3f3a3e0d2a0c91e5b164b9eeeeeb6cf76b44952642a47.png)\n\nIn the figure,  $z$ is the length of the ladder and $x$ the distance of the bottom of the ladder from the wall. The quantity $z=2$ does not change with time because the length of the ladder is fixed.\n\nOur job is to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the bottom of the ladder is pushed against the wall at a rate of $\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9abef4d4-d050-57eb-8e0a-61634d35af20" :tutorial/math-academy-id 503 :tutorial/title "Calculating Rates of Varying Angles Using Trigonometry"} :step/id #uuid "70026486-4a5b-5b48-bfe7-c78c5c312ef0" :step/math-academy-id 939 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065329}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064386 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064030 :question/id #uuid "7c740ffe-3391-52cc-b8fd-7fb9a54e58cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-234"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfd57a01-349d-5632-96a3-7f17a8e484d2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554622}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg as an Angle Varies"} :step/id #uuid "804402e9-5068-546f-a006-16edfa5e5a69" :step/math-academy-id 940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065177}}] :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates Using Trigonometry" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064455}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554713} :activity/id #uuid "ca108108-17d4-5f4b-8827-323beb81ff84" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049168 :topic/id #uuid "e02cd9a7-60ac-54e8-865a-ffe6fe24b944" :topic/math-academy-id 369} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acd652e-3691-5e84-bd19-a5ecf963137a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553891} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1bfd8d1-d406-5259-8076-e675c9f53bf2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d00df18b-1c5e-50a7-89f5-e1bc96838901" :question/math-academy-id "q-314883" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7de8ecd3d178cf07377aef5ca96ce28f638700e6284d151e3578ba11ed87327a.png)\n\nEthan notices a drone descending. He estimates that the distance between his location and the point on the ground directly beneath the drone is $150\\,\\text{ft}.$ If the angle of elevation $\\theta $ between him and the drone decreases at a rate of $0.1\\,\\text{rad/s},$ at what rate is its altitude decreasing when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}?$ Assume that the rate of decreasing altitude is **positive** and do not round the answer.\n\nThe drone's altitude is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/50/5024a642ac27698d848577165b08e3158d3248d50c064a79432fcad6e2df90d5.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone’s altitude, and $x$ is the distance between Ethan and the shadow. The quantity $x=150\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between Ethan and the shadow is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{150} \\\\ y & =150\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(150\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-0.1$ (negative because the angle is decreasing) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})(-0.1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150(4)(-0.1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is decreasing at a rate of $60\\,\\text{ft/s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "3a9e38c3-b897-5f5d-93dc-fef0dc09243e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554678}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25909b6a-2bda-5136-9440-1e737f4b1063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}\\sin^2\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553935}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553935} :answer-field/id #uuid "075eec91-479f-514d-b496-401b7ceb517c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70ef2d3a-cac0-51c8-b6cc-9ac0c81e8893" :question/math-academy-id "q-343183" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c410aeaa3ceeafe3b072233a51020594370431e35244ca240637c5b124807983.png)\n\nA firefighter $F$ is lowering a rescue basket $B$ from a helicopter. The basket stays $40\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground while it moves horizontally toward the firefighter at a speed of $12\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ Find, in radians per second as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the angle $\\theta $ between the cable and the horizontal is increasing.\n\nThe angle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cf/cfac2040d258398133397c5322e650bf60f59c7c372c97bfecec654eb3a4a278.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the basket's height, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the firefighter and the basket. The quantity $y=40$ feet does not change with time because the basket height remains the same.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{40}{x} \\\\ x & =40\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(40\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-12\\,\\text{ft/s}$ (negative because the horizontal distance is decreasing). Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =(-40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{12}{40} & =({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{40}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{10}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{10}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{rad/s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "663845a1-8a70-5c83-82d2-15fcdebd3d49"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33182b33-0b36-5640-a4c9-778cbc8910ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.55\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553959} {:answer/id #uuid "913b0f46-1aad-5da8-b8dd-1bf4b4bb9711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.78\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554184} {:answer/id #uuid "b840f7bb-0678-5531-ad85-8cea6f5889e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.84\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554270} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8772f4-5572-5416-89ab-0f1ffa36973b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.62\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554322} {:answer/id #uuid "e24d129b-6aba-55f9-bf48-3093ebfec375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.93\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554322} :answer-field/id #uuid "c44dc184-d6a9-51f1-aaf9-4f2394dbe32b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "847493a1-e66a-5ae4-9cf4-e1e68a84c19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-121713" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ecb02db9984510219c6209392c9f35df52eca11dacc802a133aa51c4e5065ede.png)\n\nA drone $D$ takes off from a point $P$ and climbs with an angle of elevation of ${60}^{∘}$ and a vertical speed of $4\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$ Assuming the angle of elevation doesn't change, calculate the actual speed of the drone in the direction it is traveling.\nRound your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "![](images/69/69314419f6e0b93236fc048c6b9b6b262b09ff2cc061996353fbe23870a12726.png)\n\nLet $y$ denote the vertical distance from the drone's projection to the starting point, and $z$ be the distance between the drone and the starting point, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={60}^{∘},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=4\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot 4≈4.62\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the speed of the drone is approximately $4.62\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9666dca7-9168-5a9d-9fb0-a3b4bc64ba0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554664}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fac3cfa-7abf-57a7-a5c4-1542f336991f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553956} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4dce0c-9b7e-5a89-af25-5aa2a3346d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554054} {:answer/id #uuid "6a41d46c-11a5-5084-856a-7e8059285981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "500\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554077} {:answer/id #uuid "ec45e093-51a8-53a6-b55a-12e8101873db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554384} {:answer/id #uuid "fe72755a-430b-5ef8-a7fc-ef1ec5c17011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554423} :answer-field/id #uuid "b75072c9-8631-52be-933c-279fbcae935a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5cbfe710-aab6-5e49-b65f-e20759621640" :question/math-academy-id "q-53189" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff93274daa7f179f86343e59b7cf277c0e70555fdfdd3cce113c3cba149b08ac.png)\n\nAn airplane $A$ is flying horizontally at a constant height of $800\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the airplane and a nearby radar station $R$ is decreasing at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second. What is the speed of the aircraft as a function of $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/29/29d06d071e6983d3487426b08d5a5548a611134dcf116674d199f6d3de03f246.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the airplane's altitude, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the radar station and the airplane. The quantity $y=800$ meters does not change with time because the airplane stays at a constant altitude.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{800}{x} \\\\ x & =800\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(800\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-800{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-0.05$ (negative because the angle is decreasing). Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-800{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-800{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )(-0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the aircraft is $40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{m/s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9b218fda-b689-564e-9378-201209ba1bc4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554718}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14580d19-87db-5577-b886-dede88420678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553909} {:answer/id #uuid "1d5b4088-b923-5d8d-b898-9ef038b7221e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553921} {:answer/id #uuid "78790129-6ad3-5b94-a1fd-e8e6c328e78d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554111} {:answer/id #uuid "9212d90c-2509-5164-b39b-daaf88c41146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554190} {:answer/id #uuid "f407d38e-a26b-5b5c-8e63-0d3d2b384765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553921} :answer-field/id #uuid "5096af22-5b72-55f8-89a3-f7336942ca60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4220ee9a-71ec-56c9-a4ec-d0480b90a44d" :question/math-academy-id "q-53199" :question/problem "![](images/06/06f965498f6415c08b32c5ae30235a8eec65d6dd67cd1f349e80bcbe88bd5495.png)\n\nA boy $B$ is flying a kite $K$ with a $20\\,\\text{m}$ length of string. If the kite is rising vertically at a constant speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ at what rate is the angle of elevation $\\theta $ increasing when $\\theta ={45}^{∘}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/23/236144ff93bd22f5c4460c31e20216c45d6f8ac85dc945321dadecc093f379e0.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $z$ is the distance between the boy and the kite. The quantity $z=20$ meters does not change with time because the string's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{20} \\\\ y & =20\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConverting the given information from degrees to radians, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{180}^{∘}}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{20} & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}$ radians per second."} :step/id #uuid "a88a66de-c5b3-5b9e-aac7-5154923ab26e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554708}}] :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates Using Trigonometry" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553859}]}
